60.body:
Any dir that is not preserved itself but is a parent of a preserved file or dir is called a scaffold path component here. Regarding the ownership and permissions of these scaffold path components, the following needs to be considered. Scaffold path components of user-specific files and dirs Parent dirs of a preserved user-specific file or dir are created with the respective user as their owner and permissions 0755. This is the case for all scaffold path components up to, but not including, the user's home dir. Example Consider the following preservation config: keepTo."/state/userdata" = { username = "alice"; dirs = [ ".local/state/nvim" ];
}; This config will cause the module to configure a bind-mount for the subdir nvim, causing its contents to be preserved. The scaffold path components .local and state will be created if necessary, but their contents are not preserved. Owner is set to alice, group to alice's primary group, i.e. users.users.alice.group and the mode is set to 0755. Scaffold path components of system-wide files and dirs For system-wide files and dirs, missing components of a preserved path that do not already exist, are created by systemd-tmpfiles with default ownership root:root and mode 0755. Should such dirs require different ownership or mode, the intended way to create and configure them is via systemd-tmpfiles directly. Example Consider a preserved file /foo/bar/baz: keepTo."/state".files = [ { file = "/foo/bar/baz"; user = "baz"; group = "baz"; };
]; This would create the file with desired ownership on both the volatile and persistent volumes. However, the parent dirs that did not exist before, i.e. /foo and /foo/bar, are created with ownership root:root and mode 0755. Keep allows the configuration of immediate parents, so the permissions for /foo/bar can be configured: keepTo."/state".files = [ { file = "/foo/bar/baz"; user = "baz"; group = "baz"; parent.enable = true; parent.user = "baz"; parent.group = "bar"; };
]; Now the parent dir /foo/bar is configured with ownership baz:bar. But the first path component /foo still has systemd-tmpfiles' default ownership and the configuration becomes quite convoluted. Solution To create or configure scaffold path components of a persisted path, systemd-tmpfiles may be used directly: # configure preservation of single file
keepTo."/state".files = [ { file = "/foo/bar/baz"; user = "baz"; group = "bar"; };
]; # create and configure parents of preserved file on the volatile volume with custom permissions
# The keep module also uses `settings.keep` here.
systemd.tmpfiles.settings.keep = { "/foo".d = { user = "foo"; group = "bar"; mode = "0775"; }; "/foo/bar".d = { user = "bar"; group = "bar"; mode = "0755"; };
}; See tmpfiles.d(5) for available configuration options.

